{
  "source": "scripts/color-taylor-script-cut-04.md",
  "wpm": 150,
  "gaps": {
    "line": 0.5,
    "paragraph": 1.2,
    "beat": 2.5
  },
  "lead": 1.8,
  "tail": 3,
  "lines": [
    {
      "id": "1.1",
      "beat": 1,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "Hey, I'm Taylor.",
      "start": 1.8,
      "end": 3,
      "gapBefore": 1.8
    },
    {
      "id": "1.1",
      "beat": 1,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "I'm a real human.",
      "start": 3.5,
      "end": 5.1,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "1.2",
      "beat": 1,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "I'm also a user experience and product designer, and I'd like to show you something I've been working on.",
      "start": 5.6,
      "end": 13.2,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "1.3",
      "beat": 1,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "It's called Color Taylor.",
      "start": 13.7,
      "end": 15.3,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "2.1",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "I've always struggled with mixing colors with RGB values, but I found hue and saturation models helpful for being able to find the colors that I want.",
      "start": 17.8,
      "end": 28.6,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "2.2",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "I have this turquoise color, but I want to get to teal, which is darker and more colorful.",
      "start": 29.1,
      "end": 36.3,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "2.2",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "How do I dial that into the RGB?",
      "start": 36.8,
      "end": 40,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "2.3",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "Once I understood how hue models and RGB work together, I could more confidently choose harmonious colors, which I could then build into larger systems.",
      "start": 40.5,
      "end": 50.5,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "2.4",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "Our focus for this presentation will be hue, saturation, and brightness.",
      "start": 51,
      "end": 55.4,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "2.5",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 5,
      "text": "But the app supports HSL as well, so you can use what's familiar to you.",
      "start": 55.9,
      "end": 61.9,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "2.6",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 6,
      "text": "Color Taylor is meant to be a playground and educational tool for how RGB and HSB work together.",
      "start": 62.4,
      "end": 69.6,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "2.7",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 7,
      "text": "You see the relationships in real time, to help better understand how colors work on your display.",
      "start": 70.1,
      "end": 76.9,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "2.8",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 8,
      "text": "I'll just move myself out of the way, for now.",
      "start": 77.4,
      "end": 81.4,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "3.1",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "The color editor on the right should be familiar to most people who have worked with creative tools.",
      "start": 83.9,
      "end": 91.1,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "3.2",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "The hexagon on the left is a different way of displaying the same information.",
      "start": 92.3,
      "end": 97.9,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "3.3",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "Hue is an angle that ranges from 0 to 360 degrees.",
      "start": 99.1,
      "end": 103.5,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "3.4",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "Red is 0...",
      "start": 104.7,
      "end": 105.9,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "3.4",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "green is 120...",
      "start": 106.4,
      "end": 107.6,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "3.4",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "and blue is 240.",
      "start": 108.1,
      "end": 109.7,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "3.5",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 5,
      "text": "Color Taylor's difference from standard sliders is that ours are oriented to their hue direction around the hex.",
      "start": 110.9,
      "end": 118.1,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "3.6",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 6,
      "text": "And in between each of these we see combinations: yellow, which is red and green combined; cyan, which is green and blue; and magenta, which is blue and red.",
      "start": 119.3,
      "end": 130.9,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "3.7",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 7,
      "text": "Not to be confused with CMYK, which is a topic for another time.",
      "start": 132.1,
      "end": 137.3,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "4.1",
      "beat": 4,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "Let's take an orange as a reference point, 30 degrees.",
      "start": 139.8,
      "end": 143.8,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "4.2",
      "beat": 4,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "When the hue is set to 30 degrees, I know that's going to be orange, because it's between red and yellow, and yellow is between red and green.",
      "start": 145,
      "end": 156.2,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "4.3",
      "beat": 4,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "So it's a combination of 2 parts red, 1 part green, 0 parts blue.",
      "start": 156.7,
      "end": 162.3,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "5.1",
      "beat": 5,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "Adding saturation increases the distance between red, green, and blue.",
      "start": 164.8,
      "end": 168.8,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "5.2",
      "beat": 5,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "Desaturating reduces the distance proportionally between these.",
      "start": 170,
      "end": 172.8,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "5.3",
      "beat": 5,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "Saturation works off the highest value.",
      "start": 174,
      "end": 176.4,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "5.3",
      "beat": 5,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "Red holds still, and green and blue move toward it or away from it.",
      "start": 176.9,
      "end": 182.5,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "5.4",
      "beat": 5,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "When all three are equal, there's no saturation, and you're left with gray.",
      "start": 183.7,
      "end": 188.9,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "5.5",
      "beat": 5,
      "line": 5,
      "text": "Notice the hue didn't change, we're still at 30, which is orange.",
      "start": 190.1,
      "end": 194.9,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "5.5",
      "beat": 5,
      "line": 5,
      "text": "Same hue, only the distance between RGB values changed.",
      "start": 195.4,
      "end": 199,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "6.1",
      "beat": 6,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "Brightness is determined by the highest color value.",
      "start": 201.5,
      "end": 204.7,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "6.2",
      "beat": 6,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "As we scale the brightness, the hue and saturation are maintained by scaling the RGB values proportionately to each other.",
      "start": 205.9,
      "end": 213.9,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "6.3",
      "beat": 6,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "Brightness scales off of zero.",
      "start": 215.1,
      "end": 217.1,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "6.3",
      "beat": 6,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "All three channels scale together, so the ratios between them stay the same.",
      "start": 217.6,
      "end": 222.8,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "6.4",
      "beat": 6,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "At a hundred percent brightness the hex is at its largest,",
      "start": 224,
      "end": 228.4,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "6.5",
      "beat": 6,
      "line": 5,
      "text": "and lowering it shrinks the size of the hex.",
      "start": 228.9,
      "end": 232.5,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "6.6",
      "beat": 6,
      "line": 6,
      "text": "Notice we stay at the hue 30 orange while moving brightness.",
      "start": 233,
      "end": 237.4,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "7.1",
      "beat": 7,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "We'll use the swatches section to show how we can use HSB to set six different tints of the same hue.",
      "start": 239.9,
      "end": 248.3,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "7.2",
      "beat": 7,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "We can use HSB to come up with a bright desaturated orange, then go down the scale until we have a darker one.",
      "start": 249.5,
      "end": 258.7,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "7.3",
      "beat": 7,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "This is hinting at what a design system might do.",
      "start": 259.9,
      "end": 263.9,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "8.1",
      "beat": 8,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "We've been exploring the playground, but if you want the classroom perspective, check out the equations section.",
      "start": 266.4,
      "end": 273.2,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "8.2",
      "beat": 8,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "It shows the math behind HSB and HSL conversions, as well as how Hex and normalized colors are calculated.",
      "start": 274.4,
      "end": 282,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "9.1",
      "beat": 9,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "Now we can move on to the demo, which gives you an outline of some of the things to look for in the Color Taylor app.",
      "start": 284.5,
      "end": 294.9,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "9.2",
      "beat": 9,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "You can find this demo the first time loading the app,",
      "start": 296.1,
      "end": 300.5,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "9.3",
      "beat": 9,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "or from the question mark button at the top of the app.",
      "start": 301.7,
      "end": 306.5,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "10.1",
      "beat": 10,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "Start with the sliders and controls you already know.",
      "start": 309,
      "end": 312.6,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "10.2",
      "beat": 10,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "Moving one slider will highlight every other slider it affects.",
      "start": 313.8,
      "end": 317.8,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "10.3",
      "beat": 10,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "Click the droplet button to switch between Source or Mixed colored sliders.",
      "start": 319,
      "end": 323.8,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "10.4",
      "beat": 10,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "Click the tags button to show HTML colors on the hex.",
      "start": 325,
      "end": 329.4,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "10.5",
      "beat": 10,
      "line": 5,
      "text": "Drag a handle on the hex and watch the RGB and HSB sliders follow.",
      "start": 330.6,
      "end": 336.2,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "10.6",
      "beat": 10,
      "line": 6,
      "text": "Having fun is the goal of the tool.",
      "start": 337.4,
      "end": 340.6,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "11.1",
      "beat": 11,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "If you use Figma, I've also made a Color Taylor plugin that you might like to check out.",
      "start": 343.1,
      "end": 350.3,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "11.2",
      "beat": 11,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "That's Color Taylor!",
      "start": 350.8,
      "end": 352,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "11.3",
      "beat": 11,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "Go play with it, and I hope it helps you see the relationship between RGB and HSB in a different light.",
      "start": 352.5,
      "end": 360.9,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "11.4",
      "beat": 11,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "If it's useful, I'd like to hear from you.",
      "start": 361.4,
      "end": 365,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "11.4",
      "beat": 11,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "Questions and feedback are welcome.",
      "start": 365.5,
      "end": 367.5,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "11.5",
      "beat": 11,
      "line": 5,
      "text": "Thanks, see ya.",
      "start": 368,
      "end": 369.2,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    }
  ],
